WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:08.500
In 1856, a scientist, named Eunice Foote,
conducted an experiment.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.097
She filled one tube with regular air, and
another with carbon dioxide, 

00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:16.539
put thermometers in them, and 
placed them in the sun.

00:00:16.539 --> 00:00:22.630
And she noticed, the tube of carbon dioxide
got a lot hotter, and stayed hot longer.

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:26.867
She published her results noting that
"an atmosphere of that gas 

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:30.540
would give to our earth 
a high temperature".

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.664
Three years later, Edwin Drake struck oil
in Western Pennsylvania.

00:00:36.664 --> 00:00:43.050
A hundred years after that first well, the
American oil industry celebrated its centennial.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:47.820
And they invited the physicist Edward Teller,
one of the inventors of the atomic bomb, 

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.910
to make a speech about the future of energy.

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.219
“We probably have to look for additional
fuel supplies,” he told the crowd.

00:00:55.219 --> 00:01:00.220
“Because the extra carbon emitted from burning
fossil fuels causes a greenhouse effect.”

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:07.430
Which, he believed, would be sufficient to
melt the ice cap and submerge New York.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:15.335
By 1965, scientists were confident
enough to formally warn U.S. President Lyndon B. Johnson

00:01:15.335 --> 00:01:20.280
A decade later, Exxon’s own scientists were
making grim predictions.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.165
By 1988, it was 
front page news.

00:01:24.165 --> 00:01:29.057
And since then, we’ve kept pumping 
carbon dioxide into the atmosphere 

00:01:29.057 --> 00:01:31.000
at an accelerating rate.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:36.330
We have a world economy today that depends
on fossil fuels for most of its energy.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.250
A third of it from oil.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:44.610
It's a tremendous irony that the very substances
that helped us achieve this level of development

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:50.188
today are, now, the very substances that endanger
the future of civilization as we know it.

00:01:50.188 --> 00:01:55.420
Governments are starting to agree that we
shouldn't let the world warm more than one-point-five

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:01.294
degrees centigrade, and we're on track 
to blow past that by twenty-thirty.

00:02:01.294 --> 00:02:04.979
So, why is it so hard to turn off the tap?

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:08.677
And can we do it in time?

00:02:08.677 --> 00:02:12.136
Industrial nations have developed a great
dependency on oil.

00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:15.069
It has added a new freedom to our lives.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:17.939
The invaluable stocks of oil 
in these exotic islands.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:21.969
Their wealth is cracking the old life of Arabia
wide open.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:27.428
Nigerian government love the oil 
more than our lives.

00:02:27.428 --> 00:02:30.969
Increasing amounts of carbon dioxide 
surround us.

00:02:30.969 --> 00:02:37.756
If man continues to abuse his environment,
Earth too may become barren.

00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:49.011
The story of oil is a story of geopolitical
clash, technological advancement

00:02:49.011 --> 00:02:51.500
and intense competition.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.824
The story of oil is a story of inequality.

00:02:54.824 --> 00:02:57.109
It’s a story of dominance.

00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:04.858
The Nigeria in which I was born in was just a
couple of years before the ending of 

00:03:04.858 --> 00:03:06.969
the British colonial rule.

00:03:06.969 --> 00:03:10.110
At the time, it was an agricultural economy.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:15.008
Cotton from the north, cacao from the west,
and rubber from the mid-west.

00:03:15.008 --> 00:03:18.120
And in the area where Nnimmo grew up, fishing.

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:26.279
The Niger Delta is an area that is crisscrossed
by water bodies, creek streams, rivers, estuaries

00:03:26.279 --> 00:03:29.739
which is the breeding ground for most fish
in the Gulf of Guinea.

00:03:29.739 --> 00:03:34.158
It was so fertile, fishermen could just 
leave their traps at high tide

00:03:34.158 --> 00:03:35.629
and pick them up at low tide.

00:03:35.629 --> 00:03:37.209
And in the evenings...

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:41.011
Children would sit around in the moonlight,
and the elders would share stories.

00:03:41.039 --> 00:03:46.579
They didn’t know they were sitting on one
of the most oil-rich regions on Earth.

00:03:46.579 --> 00:03:53.422
Until the British granted Shell and BP an
exclusive permit to explore for oil.

00:03:53.422 --> 00:03:57.533
They struck black gold in 1956. 

00:03:57.533 --> 00:04:02.979
Nigerians were extremely hopeful that the
discovery of oil in their would bring about

00:04:02.979 --> 00:04:07.913
positive changes in their economic wellbeing,
in the health conditions of the people

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.079
in terms of employment and everything.

00:04:10.079 --> 00:04:14.319
And just a few years later, Nigeria won independence.

00:04:14.319 --> 00:04:18.289
The future looked bright.

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:21.970
After all, fossil fuels had transformed 
other countries.

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.610
The world’s wealthiest nations had once
been much poorer.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:30.487
The amount of work a person could do was the
amount they could do with their hands

00:04:30.487 --> 00:04:34.000
possibly helped by a horse or mule.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.280
Coal was the first discovery 
that changed all that.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.500
Ancient organisms in oceans and swamps had
soaked up the power of the sun.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:48.336
Their fossils compressed over millions of
years into coal, and a mile or more down

00:04:48.336 --> 00:04:51.689
into natural gas and crude oil.

00:04:51.689 --> 00:04:57.077
Burning coal, this time-capsule of the sun’s
energy, helped Britain become 

00:04:57.077 --> 00:04:59.215
the first industrialized nation

00:04:59.215 --> 00:05:02.793
and the most powerful empire 
the word had ever seen.

00:05:02.793 --> 00:05:04.689
And then, oil came along.

00:05:04.689 --> 00:05:07.370
And that stared off this kind of boom.

00:05:08.673 --> 00:05:12.403
It was discovered that gasoline, which had
been kind of this waste product 

00:05:12.403 --> 00:05:17.097
when they refined oil, was actually 
a very good fuel for cars.

00:05:17.097 --> 00:05:24.939
Oil was the most energy-packed liquid source
of power that you could get your hands on.

00:05:24.939 --> 00:05:28.632
Right from the very beginning, it was very
important to the British navy who wanted 

00:05:28.632 --> 00:05:32.090
to have access to British-controlled oil.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:38.300
It started as a syndicate of private investors
that went on a journey and an adventure 

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:43.650
to find oil the in the foothills of the 
Zagros mountains in Persia.

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:47.509
That was the start of the 
Anglo-Persian Oil Company.

00:05:47.509 --> 00:05:49.930
Later renamed, British Petroleum.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:53.960
And they were just in time.

00:05:53.960 --> 00:06:00.889
The first world war began with cavalry charges
and people on horses, and it ended with airplanes,

00:06:00.889 --> 00:06:03.350
with tanks, with trucks.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:08.790
When the allied navy switched to using oil
instead of coal, those ships could go further

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.469
before refueling.

00:06:11.469 --> 00:06:15.639
Oil put the world in motion.

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:20.370
People were finding newfound freedom driving
all over the place, flying all over the place.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:24.000
It’s pulled millions of people out of poverty.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.819
What oil did was really create 
the modern world.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:28.610
Power plants were built.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:29.979
Roads were built.

00:06:29.979 --> 00:06:31.424
Gas stations were built.

00:06:31.424 --> 00:06:32.830
Refineries were built.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:36.719
Everybody wanted investment in oil and gas.

00:06:36.719 --> 00:06:38.729
But the profits were lopsided.

00:06:38.729 --> 00:06:42.304
The ocean of crude oil underneath 
Persia’s desert led the way 

00:06:42.304 --> 00:06:44.470
to the Middle East oil boom.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:48.000
Iran was making just a fraction of the profits
from their own oil

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.169
while the British raked in the rest.

00:06:50.169 --> 00:06:53.529
And they decided they were sick of that deal.

00:06:53.529 --> 00:06:58.810
Long-smoldering Iranian nationalists made
clear their intention to seize the oil industry

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:01.543
and expropriate the British company.

00:07:01.543 --> 00:07:06.000
So, in 1953, Britain and the U.S. 
engineered a coup, 

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.386
overthrowing Iran’s democratically elected leader 
to install the Shah.

00:07:10.386 --> 00:07:17.979
Because they felt that he was more amenable
to having a great relationship with the West.

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:21.036
Which is how a young Lord Brown ended up there.

00:07:21.036 --> 00:07:26.789
Yeah, I spent many years as a child in Iran
when my father was working in the oil industry.

00:07:26.789 --> 00:07:30.349
And when he turned eighteen, 
he started working for BP himself.

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:35.909
I joined the oil industry believing 
it was a place where you could solve problems

00:07:35.909 --> 00:07:38.159
that no one had solved before.

00:07:38.159 --> 00:07:41.759
How could you use oil to go further and farther?

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:47.509
At the time, BP was one of seven companies
from just three countries that controlled

00:07:47.509 --> 00:07:51.925
85% of the world’s oil reserves.

00:07:51.925 --> 00:07:59.710
And, over in Nigeria, they quickly learned
that oil didn’t mean prosperity for everyone.

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:05.080
Under colonial rule, the British had forced
diverse states into a single nation.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:10.639
And, after independence, Nimmo's home region
announced it was seceding.

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:15.689
As a young child, I did not fully understand
what was at stake.

00:08:15.689 --> 00:08:20.270
To me, the most exciting thing was that there
was going to be a new nation called Biafra.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:25.575
But this region encompassed most of 
the Niger Delta and its oil reserves.

00:08:25.575 --> 00:08:30.720
So, when the Nigerian government declared
war, the British gave their support.

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:33.320
My village was, more or less, a warfront.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.729
The government blockaded the region.

00:08:35.729 --> 00:08:40.708
It’s estimated that more than a million
civilians died of starvation.

00:08:40.708 --> 00:08:46.440
I still hear voices in my head, sometimes,
of people asking for help, crying for food.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.150
It’s not something that you 
forget in a hurry.

00:08:49.150 --> 00:08:52.339
Biafra surrendered in 1970.

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:59.093
The next year, Nigeria joined OPEC, an alliance
of oil-producing nations that wanted to 

00:08:59.093 --> 00:09:02.248
take back control of their resources.

00:09:02.248 --> 00:09:07.600
And, in the 70s, they wielded their
power, raising oil prices, with some countries,

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:10.360
boycotting the U.S. for their 
military support of Israel.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:15.008
It suddenly turned into a crisis and a shock
to the political order.

00:09:15.008 --> 00:09:17.529
Gasoline stations ran dry.

00:09:17.529 --> 00:09:19.657
Airlines cut back flight schedules.

00:09:19.657 --> 00:09:21.630
Factories were forced to close.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:26.062
And, in 1979, when the Iranians
overthrew the Shah 

00:09:26.062 --> 00:09:28.215
and took back control of their oil

00:09:28.215 --> 00:09:31.016
prices went through the roof again.

00:09:31.016 --> 00:09:33.960
And while that was bad for oil-consumers...

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:34.960
Oh, it's ridiculous, you know.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:35.960
You know, it's...

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:37.329
You just don't know where it's gonna stop.

00:09:37.329 --> 00:09:39.821
It was great for oil producers.

00:09:39.821 --> 00:09:43.500
Nigeria became one ofthe wealthiest
countries in Africa.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:50.393
But after that 70s boom, oil prices
crashed, and so did Nigeria’s economy.

00:09:50.393 --> 00:09:57.510
The idea of the resource curse is that countries
don't necessarily do better just because they

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.940
have an abundance of natural resources.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.680
It can throw off the currency valuation.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.509
Make other industries less competitive.

00:10:06.509 --> 00:10:09.540
And cause economic turmoil and corruption.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:12.009
The problem is not the resource.

00:10:12.009 --> 00:10:14.660
The problem is how the resource is exploited.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.750
It's one thing for a country to get oil revenues.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.779
It's another thing is who gets the money 
and where does it go?

00:10:20.779 --> 00:10:24.589
A large chunk of that has been taken out by
transnational corporations.

00:10:24.589 --> 00:10:30.490
And the money that stays goes to the Nigerian
National Petroleum Corporation, which is owned

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:35.350
by the government, and is also in charge of
regulating the country’s oil industry.

00:10:35.350 --> 00:10:38.970
So, we have an operator who is also a regulator.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:44.870
And since independence, billions have disappeared.

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:49.469
That level of corruption, it corrupts, not
just people economically

00:10:49.469 --> 00:10:52.170
it corrupts the political system.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.730
And there were other costs.

00:10:54.730 --> 00:11:00.200
In the Niger Delta, over 50 years of spillage
has created 27,000 miles 

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:02.330
of toxic oil swamps.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:06.130
Kids are swimming in water 
covered in crude oil.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:12.370
Life expectancy is at 41 years, maybe
one of the lowest in the entire world.

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.220
Fishermen can no longer just leave their traps
at high tide.

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:21.770
You have fisher folks who go into the rivers
and toil all day, all night, and catch nothing.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:24.000
And no more moonlit nights.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:30.336
The gas flares set up by the oil corporations
run 24 hours every day.

00:11:30.336 --> 00:11:37.199
And on top of all that, Nigeria is a hot,
dry country which means it’s more sensitive

00:11:37.199 --> 00:11:39.050
to rising temperatures.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:46.759
And, as of 2020, the global temperature
has increased by more than one degree centigrade.

00:11:46.759 --> 00:11:51.040
Heat waves are getting stronger, more frequent
and more deadly.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.790
It is powering hurricanes that intensify more
quickly.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:58.380
Wildfires are burning much greater area.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:02.089
Climate change is not responding to our annual
emissions, what we’re putting out this year.

00:12:02.089 --> 00:12:04.899
It’s responding to our cumulative emissions.

00:12:04.899 --> 00:12:07.680
And so, the rich countries caused the problems.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.089
We are the ones who put out all these 
carbon emissions over all these years.

00:12:12.089 --> 00:12:15.709
But developing countries are facing 
the brunt of the cost.

00:12:15.709 --> 00:12:19.890
It’s already pushed millions of people 
to flee their homes.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:24.310
The clock is ticking, and we can really wonder
whether there's any hope that we can pull

00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:28.100
this off or whether we've come to the precipice
as, as a human race.

00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:29.190
What’s at risk?

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.259
Not the planet, it will survive.

00:12:31.259 --> 00:12:33.949
What’s at risk is us.

00:12:33.949 --> 00:12:38.770
The world emits around fifty-billion tons
of greenhouse gases a year

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:40.574
more than it ever has.

00:12:40.574 --> 00:12:46.120
And governments agree we need to 
get to net zero by 2050.

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:47.791
And achieve carbon neutrality.

00:12:47.791 --> 00:12:50.480
We’re gonna move to net zero 
in a transition in all countries.

00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:53.060
Strong aspiration to reach net zero...

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:54.589
Significantly reducing emissions.

00:12:54.589 --> 00:12:57.040
Legislation, uh, for net zero.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:03.160
Nigeria has rolled out institutional frameworks
to cut emissions by 20%.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:08.209
This issue is not like the Coronavirus
where you need one vaccine to deal with

00:13:08.209 --> 00:13:10.440
one virus and its variants.

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.009
This is a very broad issue that needs 
lots of solutions.

00:13:14.009 --> 00:13:19.389
And it's going to require a lot of technology
that really hasn't been developed yet.

00:13:19.389 --> 00:13:22.015
But there have been dramatic changes.

00:13:22.015 --> 00:13:26.250
Wind and solar power are now cheaper 
than coal in a lot of countries.

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:28.934
Battery technology is improving rapidly.

00:13:28.934 --> 00:13:32.930
Governments are investing in more hydropower
and nuclear plants.

00:13:32.930 --> 00:13:35.250
Electric cars are getting cheaper every year.

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:40.680
And for long-haul ships and planes, engineers
are working on biofuels and liquid hydrogen.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.740
And people are working on solutions 
for every piece of this pie.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:48.750
And the current goal isn’t to get to 
zero carbon emissions.

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.976
People are targeting net-zero.

00:13:51.976 --> 00:13:52.873
Net-zero.

00:13:52.873 --> 00:13:57.240
They’re saying, we will produce carbon,
but we will off-set it.

00:13:57.240 --> 00:13:59.649
By restoring forests, wetlands.

00:13:59.649 --> 00:14:01.550
Techniques in the ocean.

00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:04.089
Which can help soak up more carbon.

00:14:04.089 --> 00:14:07.440
Or carbon-capture technology, 
which is still expensive.

00:14:08.633 --> 00:14:12.029
And that’s an issue with 
a lot of these solutions.

00:14:12.029 --> 00:14:15.190
So many governments are trying 
to tip the balance.

00:14:15.190 --> 00:14:21.250
More than 40 counties have a price on carbon
to make burning fossil fuels more costly.

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:25.876
In over the past decade, the U.S. has been
moving from coal to natural gas

00:14:25.876 --> 00:14:28.170
the result of fracking.

00:14:28.170 --> 00:14:32.152
The U.S. went from being the 
world's largest importer of oil 

00:14:32.152 --> 00:14:34.899
to the world's largest producer of oil.

00:14:34.899 --> 00:14:41.575
And the natural gas plants the U.S. has been
building, are major investments in a fossil fuel future.

00:14:41.575 --> 00:14:44.660
But it’s helped the country
significantly reduce emissions.

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:48.019
And the emissions are dropping in Europe too.

00:14:48.019 --> 00:14:50.389
But global emissions are not.

00:14:50.389 --> 00:14:54.228
When you look at where emissions are growing
right now, all of that is happening 

00:14:54.228 --> 00:14:56.750
in the developing world.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:15:00.494
Even if the United States and Europe all work
together to fix the problem, 

00:15:00.494 --> 00:15:04.011
we’re still not there unless we bring 
the developing world along 

00:15:04.011 --> 00:15:07.370
because that’s where the emissions of the future are.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:13.019
Without keeping emissions down 
in the developing world, we’ll all fry.

00:15:13.019 --> 00:15:18.837
As a Nigerian and as an African, it's very,
very tough to see people living in extreme poverty

00:15:18.837 --> 00:15:22.595
and tell them that, you know, let’s
wait a few years till we get 

00:15:22.595 --> 00:15:25.846
the best possible solution 
to get you out of that.

00:15:25.846 --> 00:15:29.624
The average young person in Africa, wants
to have the same amount of energy 

00:15:29.624 --> 00:15:31.700
as the average young person in America.

00:15:31.700 --> 00:15:36.442
They want to have the same type of opportunities,
and energy is that golden thread that

00:15:36.442 --> 00:15:39.424
hinders people to reach their full potential.

00:15:39.424 --> 00:15:42.677
While Nigeria’s land is energy rich...

00:15:42.677 --> 00:15:47.050
It's the country that has the largest 
energy access deficit in the world.

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.981
In a country of, around, two hundred million people

00:15:49.981 --> 00:15:53.730
almost half don't have access to electricity.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:54.920
And for those who do...

00:15:54.920 --> 00:16:00.510
Every day at nine o’clock in the morning,
all the power supply goes off where I live.

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:06.130
It comes back at two PM and then it goes off
again at eight PM.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:11.170
So, most businesses rely on diesel-powered
generators to keep the lights on.

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:15.589
There’s eight hundred million people around
the world that do not have access to energy.

00:16:15.589 --> 00:16:19.480
And to have enough energy to live 
a full and dignified life.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.199
It’s not an inconvenience.

00:16:21.199 --> 00:16:26.410
It’s a difference between life and death
for a lot of people.

00:16:26.410 --> 00:16:31.590
They need to be able to develop, to have electricity
and infrastructure like we do.

00:16:31.590 --> 00:16:34.970
But today, we know that there’s better ways
to do that than the ways we did it two or

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:35.980
three hundred years ago.

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:37.459
So, you hear about leapfrogging.

00:16:37.459 --> 00:16:43.180
That developing countries can just jump over
the technologies that the wealthy world used

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:44.420
to get wealthy.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.041
Instead, they’ll develop based on renewables.

00:16:46.041 --> 00:16:53.589
There’s a bit of a hypocrisy with developed
countries asking countries that currently

00:16:53.589 --> 00:16:58.788
do not have that much money to leapfrog and
transition out of something 

00:16:58.788 --> 00:17:00.149
that they’re still doing.

00:17:00.149 --> 00:17:06.000
Let’s say I have an Auntie in Lagos Island,
and she takes a public bus from home 

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:07.920
to her business every single day.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:11.651
She's been saving up money 
to buy a little two-door car

00:17:11.651 --> 00:17:14.130
which probably runs on diesel or petrol.

00:17:14.130 --> 00:17:18.070
And she says, well, I don't have any money
to buy an electric car and you say no, 

00:17:18.070 --> 00:17:21.370
you have to continue on the bus 
till you get the electric car.

00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:25.450
That is what energy transition 
looks like now in Africa.

00:17:25.450 --> 00:17:29.280
Before you can tell the developing world,
you know, don’t use fossil

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:32.820
don't use coal, you have to have 
financing behind that.

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:37.679
For Africa to actually transition, experts
say it would take an investment 

00:17:37.679 --> 00:17:39.890
of $70 billion every year.

00:17:39.890 --> 00:17:45.450
The global North should pay a climate-debt
for the exploitation that has gone on 

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:47.560
for so many years.

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:52.370
Those who created the problem, have a duty
to invest in making this happen.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:57.600
And when global leaders met 
in Paris in 2015, they agreed.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:03.470
The landmark Paris Climate Accord included
something called the Green Climate Fund

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:07.660
a way for wealthy countries to help 
developing nations transition.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:11.790
They pledged to give a hundred billion a year
by 2020.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.260
And we are nowhere near getting that.

00:18:14.260 --> 00:18:18.400
They’ve fallen short by ninety billion.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:23.580
Some wealthier countries are investing billions
in clean energy projects across Africa.

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.550
But they’re investing even more 
in fossil fuels.

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:31.940
In 2020, Britain’s Prime Minister
addressed a UK-Africa summit.

00:18:31.940 --> 00:18:36.420
There’s no point in the UK reducing 
the amount of coal we burn 

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:42.696
if we then trundle over to Africa and line our
pockets by encouraging African states 

00:18:42.696 --> 00:18:44.760
to use more of it.

00:18:44.760 --> 00:18:45.760
Is there?

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:50.444
But days later, it came out that 90% of the energy deals 
that Britain had made that week

00:18:50.444 --> 00:18:52.690
were in fossil fuel projects.

00:18:52.690 --> 00:18:55.190
And the pattern continues.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:59.030
China has made wind and solar technologies
much cheaper.

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:01.850
But they're also still investing in coal plants.

00:19:01.850 --> 00:19:06.923
And recently, the U.S. has invested $9 billion
in fossil fuels around the world

00:19:06.923 --> 00:19:09.780
most in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:14.840
While the streets of wealthy countries are
getting cleaner, with cars that are more fuel-efficient

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:21.028
or electric, a lot of these old, fuel-guzzling
models aren’t vanishing from the face of the Earth.

00:19:21.028 --> 00:19:25.420
They’re exported to countries
like Nigeria, because they’re the only kinds

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:27.780
of cars most people there can afford.

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:30.560
Africa is basically seen as a dumping ground
for technologies.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:35.350
And though Nigeria is the largest oil producer
on the continent, the few refineries they have

00:19:35.350 --> 00:19:37.760
are closed or dysfunctional.

00:19:37.760 --> 00:19:43.590
So, they export their crude oil around the
world and import most of their fuel from 

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:45.740
the Netherlands and Belgium.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:48.490
But it’s not the same stuff that they burn.

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:54.704
Investigators found that diesel samples contained
sulfur levels 204 times what’s allowed

00:19:54.704 --> 00:19:57.513
under European fuel standards.

00:19:57.513 --> 00:20:01.100
There’s a lot being invested in 
destruction in the world today.

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:07.600
The challenge the world faces now is to move
from a system of inequality to a system that

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:10.544
is more just and more fair.

00:20:10.544 --> 00:20:15.611
I certainly feel ownership for both 
the benefits of oil and gas

00:20:15.611 --> 00:20:18.747
and the issues including climate change.

00:20:18.747 --> 00:20:24.192
Back in 1997, Lord Browne made a speech 
that shocked the oil industry.

00:20:24.192 --> 00:20:28.670
There is a discernable 
human influence on the climate.

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:35.470
The oil world reacted badly, and declared
that I had, quote, left the church.

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:41.050
But now, the world’s oil giants are also
acknowledging we need to get to net zero.

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:44.790
They see which way the political winds are
blowing and they’re going with them.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:50.050
They must contribute to the solution, not
just hope the word sorry can get you out of

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:51.660
the penalty box.

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:53.960
Most companies have a choice to make.

00:20:53.960 --> 00:20:58.200
But overall, these oil companies 
have chosen oil.

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:02.840
Renewables make up less than one percent 
of their investments.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:08.250
One report estimates that in 2030,
most of the world’s oil giants will actually

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:12.040
be producing more oil than they do today.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:17.900
And while private companies once ruled the
world, government owned ones now produce 

00:21:17.900 --> 00:21:19.620
half the world’s oil and gas.

00:21:19.620 --> 00:21:24.000
And many of their economies are 
largely dependent on them.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:28.530
Countries that are very economically dependent
on oil, face a real challenge.

00:21:28.530 --> 00:21:31.540
Their production tends to be cheaper 
than anybody else’s.

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:39.300
So, they'll probably be the last people, 
as it were, to turn out the lights on this industry.

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:45.330
The $88 trillion world economy has
been based on an energy system in which oil

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:47.700
has the preeminent role.

00:21:47.700 --> 00:21:49.360
Other energy transitions took centuries.

00:21:49.360 --> 00:21:51.850
This is meant to happen in 30 years.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:54.820
I expect oil will be around for 
quite a long time.

00:21:54.820 --> 00:22:00.370
But it will be used by people who have
no option but to use oil.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:04.740
Rich countries who are historically responsible
for the greatest proportion of carbon emissions,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:08.320
they have the greatest responsibility 
to act first and most.

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:12.530
So, there’s this issue of fairness, and,
in a sense, everyone is right here.

00:22:12.530 --> 00:22:14.010
But it doesn’t really matter.

00:22:14.010 --> 00:22:18.667
We all need to work on this together whether
or not its fair in any sense.

00:22:18.667 --> 00:22:23.050
Developing countries are saying yes, we want
to be part of this, we want to transition,

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:25.260
but we really need the help.

00:22:25.260 --> 00:22:30.770
We cannot achieve our climate goals 
if we don’t achieve universal access for everybody.

00:22:30.770 --> 00:22:35.365
The story of energy, climate change, and development
have to be one of the same.

00:22:35.365 --> 00:22:40.040
30 years from now, the world will look different, 
but how much it will change

00:22:40.040 --> 00:22:44.890
and how different it will look that’s still
very hard to see.

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:48.850
It is sometimes difficult to dream about 
the future and the way to get there.

00:22:48.850 --> 00:22:53.410
But a new system is possible.

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:54.820
And that is where my hope is.

